[section:multilevel_coordinate_search Multilevel Coordinate Search]

[heading Synopsis]

``
    #include <boost/math/optimization/multilevel_coordinate_search.hpp>

    namespace boost::math::optimization {

    template <typename ArgumentContainer> struct multilevel_coordinate_search_parameters {
        using Real = typename ArgumentContainer::value_type;
        ArgumentContainer lower_bounds;
        ArgumentContainer upper_bounds;
        ArgumentContainer const * initial_guess = nullptr;
        size_t max_function_calls = 0;
        size_t max_level = 0;
        bool local_search = true;
        size_t max_local_search_steps = 50;
        size_t line_search_points = 15;
    };

    template <typename ArgumentContainer>
    void validate_multilevel_coordinate_search_parameters(multilevel_coordinate_search_parameters<ArgumentContainer> const &params);

    template <typename ArgumentContainer, class Func>
    ArgumentContainer multilevel_coordinate_search(const Func cost_function,
                                                   multilevel_coordinate_search_parameters<ArgumentContainer> const &params,
                                                   std::invoke_result_t<Func, ArgumentContainer> target_value
                                                     = std::numeric_limits<std::invoke_result_t<Func, ArgumentContainer>>::quiet_NaN(),
                                                   std::atomic<bool> *cancellation = nullptr,
                                                   std::atomic<std::invoke_result_t<Func, ArgumentContainer>> *current_minimum_cost = nullptr,
                                                   std::vector<std::pair<ArgumentContainer, std::invoke_result_t<Func, ArgumentContainer>>> *queries = nullptr);

    } // namespaces
``

The `multilevel_coordinate_search` function searches for a global minimum of a function on a box,
using the multilevel coordinate search (MCS) algorithm of Huyer and Neumaier.
MCS splits the box into smaller boxes along one coordinate at a time, each with a base point at which the function has been evaluated.
Every box has a level, which measures how often it has been split; a sweep through the levels splits the box with the smallest function value on each level,
so that large unexplored boxes (global search) and boxes with small function values (local search) are both refined.
Boxes that reach the maximum level start local searches, which build quadratic models of the function by coordinate searches and minimize them over the box.

Unlike differential evolution, jSO, random search, and CMA-ES, MCS is deterministic: it needs no random number generator and always makes the same function calls.
It is single-threaded, and is at its best on smooth functions in a modest number of dimensions, where it often finds the global minimum in a few hundred function calls.


[heading Parameters]

* `lower_bounds`: A container representing the lower bounds of the optimization space along each dimension.
    The `.size()` of the bounds should return the dimension of the problem.
* `upper_bounds`: A container representing the upper bounds of the optimization space along each dimension.
    It should have the same size as `lower_bounds`, and each element must be strictly greater than the corresponding element of `lower_bounds`.
* `initial_guess`: An optional point in the box which is used as the base point of the initial box.
    It is the first point evaluated. Defaults to the midpoint of the box.
* `max_function_calls`: Defaults to 1000 times the dimension.
* `max_level`: The number of levels /s/[sub max]; boxes that reach this level are no longer split.
    Defaults to 5/n/ + 10, where /n/ is the dimension, as in Section 7.3 of the paper. It must not be 1.
* `local_search`: Whether to start local searches from the boxes that reach the maximum level. Defaults to true.
* `max_local_search_steps`: The maximum number of iterations of each local search. Defaults to 50.
* `line_search_points`: The maximum number of function values in a single line search of the local search. Defaults to 15, and must be at least 3.

`validate_multilevel_coordinate_search_parameters` throws a `std::domain_error` if the bounds or initial guess are invalid,
and a `std::invalid_argument` if `max_level` or `line_search_points` is out of range.
`multilevel_coordinate_search` calls it before the first function evaluation.

[heading The function]

``
template <typename ArgumentContainer, class Func>
ArgumentContainer multilevel_coordinate_search(const Func cost_function,
                                               multilevel_coordinate_search_parameters<ArgumentContainer> const &params,
                                               std::invoke_result_t<Func, ArgumentContainer> target_value
                                                 = std::numeric_limits<std::invoke_result_t<Func, ArgumentContainer>>::quiet_NaN(),
                                               std::atomic<bool> *cancellation = nullptr,
                                               std::atomic<std::invoke_result_t<Func, ArgumentContainer>> *current_minimum_cost = nullptr,
                                               std::vector<std::pair<ArgumentContainer, std::invoke_result_t<Func, ArgumentContainer>>> *queries = nullptr)
``

Parameters:

* `cost_function`: The cost function to be minimized. A NaN return value is treated as +[infin].
* `params`: The parameters to the algorithm as described above.
* `target_value`: An optional value that, if reached, stops the optimization.
    This is the most robust way to terminate the calculation, but in most cases the optimal value of the cost function is unknown.
        If it is, use it! Physical considerations can often be used to find optimal values for cost functions.
* `cancellation`: An optional atomic boolean to allow the user to stop the computation and gracefully return the best result found up to that point.
    It is checked before every function call.
* `current_minimum_cost`: An optional atomic variable to store the current minimum cost during optimization.
    This allows developers to (e.g.) plot the progress of the minimization over time and in conjunction with the `cancellation` argument allow halting the computation when the progress stagnates.
* `queries`: An optional vector to store intermediate results during optimization.
    This is useful for debugging and perhaps volume rendering of the objective function after the calculation is complete.

Returns:

The `ArgumentContainer` corresponding to the minimum cost found by the optimization.

[heading Termination]

As in the paper, there is no convergence criterion.
The computation stops when the `target_value` is reached, when `max_function_calls` calls have been made, when `cancellation` is set,
or when every box has reached `max_level`.
In practice the budget is usually what stops it, so if the minimum value is known, pass it as `target_value`.

[heading Implementation notes]

The global part (the initialization procedure, splitting by rank and by expected gain, and the sweeps through the levels) follows Sections 3 and 4 of the paper,
and the local search and the "shopping basket" of local minimizers follow Section 5.
There are two departures from the paper in the local search:
the univariate line searches are our own (extrapolation until the best point is bracketed, then safeguarded parabolic interpolation) rather than the authors' `gls`,
and the quadratic models are minimized over the box by a small bound-constrained quadratic minimizer
(sweeps of exact coordinate minimization, each followed by a Newton step on the free variables via a Cholesky factorization,
or a step along a direction of negative curvature when the factorization fails) rather than MINQ.
Like MINQ, it finds a local minimizer of a possibly indefinite quadratic.
As in Section 4 of the paper, each box stores its history (its parent, the splitting index and the new values along it) rather than its bounds,
so the memory used is proportional to the number of function calls, not to the number of calls times the dimension;
the bounds and the data for the separable quadratic model are recovered by going back through the ancestors of the box when it is split or starts a local search.

[h4:examples Examples]

The six-hump camel function, one of the Dixon-Szego test functions of the paper, has two global minima with value -1.0316284534898774 on the box \[-3, 3\]×\[-2, 2\]:

``
#include <boost/math/optimization/multilevel_coordinate_search.hpp>
#include <array>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    using boost::math::optimization::multilevel_coordinate_search;
    using boost::math::optimization::multilevel_coordinate_search_parameters;
    using ArgType = std::array<double, 2>;
    auto camel = [](ArgType const & v) {
        double x = v[0];
        double y = v[1];
        return (4 - 2.1*x*x + x*x*x*x/3)*x*x + x*y + (-4 + 4*y*y)*y*y;
    };
    multilevel_coordinate_search_parameters<ArgType> params;
    params.lower_bounds = {-3, -2};
    params.upper_bounds = {3, 2};
    auto x = multilevel_coordinate_search(camel, params);
    std::cout << "f(" << x[0] << ", " << x[1] << ") = " << camel(x) << "\n";
}
``

[h4:references References]

* W. Huyer and A. Neumaier, ['Global optimization by multilevel coordinate search.] Journal of Global Optimization, vol. 14, pp. 331-355, 1999, doi: 10.1023/A:1008382309369. [@https://arnold-neumaier.at/ms/mcs.pdf Preprint].

[endsect] [/section:multilevel_coordinate_search]
